      "narrative": "Gallox Semiconductors is a Seed-stage semiconductor company founded by Jon McCandless and Debdeep Jena that has raised $5M+. The Chips & Semiconductors sector comprises 12 same-sector private peers (including Cerebras, Credo Semiconductor, and SambaNova Systems) and 3 public comparables (ALAB, NVDA, AMD).\n\nDue to data constraints, only one valuation method yielded a reliable result. Stage-progression analysis applies a 2.5x step-up multiplier typical of Seed-to-next-stage advancement to the $5M raised proxy, producing an implied valuation of $12M. Peer-median analysis was inapplicable: while 12 same-sector peers exist, fewer than 3 met narrow filters on stage, adjacency, or valuation tier. Public-comp anchoring also could not be executed; the three public semiconductors trade at a median 21.0x P/S, but Gallox's revenue\u2014standard practice for private companies\u2014is not on file.\n\nThe triangulation supports a valuation range of $11M to $14M, with a median of $12M. This single-method result carries material uncertainty. The range reflects only stage-typical step-up mechanics and does not incorporate peer-relative positioning or public market anchoring. Key unknowns include: revenue traction and burn rate, which would enable peer matching and public-comp application; technical differentiation versus the identified peer set; and customer pipeline maturity. Validation of this range requires either disclosure of operating metrics or identification of narrower seed-stage semiconductor comparables with recent funding data.",
