      "narrative": "Poolside, a Series B artificial intelligence and software company founded by Jason Warner and Eiso Kant, is evaluated against a comp universe of 9 private peers and 1 public comparable (Palantir). The company has raised $626M+ to date, establishing a meaningful funding baseline for stage-relative valuation.\n\nTwo valuation methods triangulate to a coherent range. The stage-progression method applies a 1.9x step-up multiplier typical of Series B advancement, yielding $1.2B. The peer-median approach references four same-sector, same-stage comparables\u2014Scale AI ($29.0B), Physical Intelligence ($5.6B), Black Forest Labs ($3.2B), and Field AI ($2.0B)\u2014producing a median valuation of $4.4B. The dispersion across peers is material: $2.0B to $29.0B, with Scale AI as a clear outlier.\n\nSynthesizing both methods produces a triangulated valuation range of $1.0B (low) to $5.1B (high), with a median estimate of $2.8B. The stage-progression method anchors the lower bound, while peer comparison accommodates the current market premium for AI software at this stage. Key uncertainties include capital deployment efficiency relative to peers, competitive positioning within the crowded AI infrastructure segment, and the degree to which recent peer valuations reflect sustainable market conditions versus cyclical enthusiasm. The wide peer range ($2.0B\u2013$29.0B) underscores valuation sensitivity to differentiation and market timing assumptions.",
