      "narrative": "Nearfield Instruments is a Series D semiconductor company founded by Roland van Vliet and Hamed Sadeghian, having raised $147.6M to date. Valuation analysis draws on a peer universe of 8 private semiconductor firms at similar or adjacent stages\u2014including Tenstorrent ($2.7B, Series D), d-Matrix ($2.0B, Series C), Etched ($5.0B, Series E), and DEEPX ($529M, Series C)\u2014alongside 3 public semiconductor comparables (NVIDIA, AMD, ALAB).\n\nTwo triangulation methods prove applicable. Stage-progression analysis, anchoring to the $148M total raised and applying a typical 1.5x Series D step-up multiplier, implies a valuation of $221M. Peer-median analysis across the 4-company private comp set yields a median valuation of $2.4B, with a range of $529M to $5.0B. Public-comp anchoring via price-to-sales cannot be applied\u2014the public semiconductor sector trades at a 21.0x median multiple, but Nearfield's revenue is not disclosed, precluding derivation.\n\nThe two applicable methods produce materially divergent results, yielding a triangulated range of $188M (low) to $2.7B (high), with a median of $1.3B. The wide dispersion reflects tension between stage-based mechanics and peer-valuation benchmarking. Key uncertainties include revenue scale relative to peers, near-term funding trajectory, and whether the company's technical positioning justifies premium or discount to the peer median. Resolution of these factors is critical to narrowing the valuation band.",
