      "narrative": "Sila Nanotechnologies, a late-stage climate and energy company, has raised $1.3B+ across its funding history. The valuation universe comprises nine Climate & Energy peers: one public company (Linamar, ticker LNZA) and eight private firms including Helion, Redwood Materials, QuantumScape, KoBold Metals, and Electric Hydrogen. However, a narrow peer-median approach proved inapplicable due to insufficient same-stage or adjacent-stage comparables within the sector that would support a robust triangulation.\n\nThe stage-progression method, anchoring to the $1.3B total raised as a proxy baseline and applying a 1.2x step-up multiplier typical of late-stage advancement, yields an implied valuation of $1.6B. This method is applicable given Sila's established fundraising history and late-stage position, though it remains backward-looking and does not incorporate operational or market-based multiples.\n\nThe analysis supports a valuation range of $1.3B to $1.8B, with a median point of $1.6B. The low end reflects the cumulative capital deployed; the median incorporates stage-typical progression; the high end represents upside within a reasonable band. Key uncertainties include the absence of comparable-company revenue multiples or EBITDA-based validation, limited public market comps in the battery materials subsector, and the absence of disclosed financial performance metrics for Sila itself. A more precise valuation would require either entry into revenue/EBITDA-multiple analysis or a narrower set of stage-equivalent private comps.",
