      "narrative": "Looking Glass, a Series A semiconductor company founded by Shawn Frayne, has raised $30M to date. The broader Chips & Semiconductors sector comprises 12 private peers and 3 public comparables (ALAB, NVDA, AMD), though the private peer set includes only adjacent-stage or dissimilar-tier companies.\n\nOf three valuation methods examined, only Stage-Progression proved applicable. This method uses the $30M total raised as a proxy for post-money valuation and applies a 2.0x step-up multiplier typical of Series A to subsequent-stage transitions, yielding a $60M implied valuation. Peer-Median analysis was inapplicable due to insufficient same-stage comparables within the Chips & Semiconductors sector\u2014while 12 peers exist in the sector, narrow filters (same stage, adjacent stage, similar valuation tier) returned fewer than the three datapoints required for a reliable median. Public-Comp Anchoring could not be executed because Looking Glass's revenue is not on file; the public semiconductor universe trades at a 21.0x P/S median, but application requires target revenue disclosure.\n\nThe analysis supports a valuation range of $51M\u2013$69M, centered on $60M, derived from stage-progression mechanics alone. The single-method constraint represents a material limitation. Key uncertainties include the appropriateness of a 2.0x multiplier for this specific company and the absence of revenue data to benchmark against public peers or to stress-test the stage-based estimate.",
