      "narrative": "Oxide Computer Company is a Series C semiconductor design company founded by Steve Tuck and Bryan Cantrill. The valuation analysis triangulates across a universe of 3 public semiconductor comparables (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private-market peers in chips and semiconductors, with particular relevance to same-stage and adjacent-stage companies including d-Matrix, DEEPX, Tenstorrent, and Positron AI.\n\nTwo quantitative methods prove applicable. The stage-progression approach, anchoring to Oxide's $389M in total capital raised and applying a 1.7x Series C step-up multiplier, yields $661M. The peer-median method draws from four directly comparable firms at Series B through Series D stages, with valuations ranging from $529M to $2.7B; the median across this set stands at $1.5B. Public comparables cannot be anchored to revenue multiples because Oxide's revenue is not disclosed, limiting applicability of the 21.0x P/S median observed in the public semiconductor sector.\n\nThese two methods triangulate to a valuation range of $562M (low) to $1.7B (high), with a median of $1.1B. The spread reflects material dispersion in peer valuations and uncertainty around capital efficiency relative to more advanced-stage competitors like Tenstorrent. Key uncertainties include customer traction depth, manufacturing roadmap credibility, and competitive positioning relative to both established semiconductor incumbents and other frontier-architecture entrants.",
